# ── Chapter 11 fidelity pins: "Where People Are Put" ─────────────────────────
#
# KIND: CONTENT pin. Run:
#   ./verify.sh --only book-1/11-where-people-are-put.pins.nibli
#   ./verify.sh          # the real check — the line above cannot see
#                        # the cross-file reconciliation or the prose guards
#
# [v0.9 2026-08-02] The "alarm that does not work" defect is REPAIRED and the
# chapter's alarm section is rewritten against the repair. The marker now reads
# a placement REPORT (put/3) against derived eligibility: no report, no alarm;
# a reported homestay placement that eligibility contradicts, and it fires.
# The closing probes exercise both halves on the same write.

# WHAT FALSE MEANS HERE — three kinds, and only one is a verdict. A FALSE on a
# corpus name this KB never mentions is real but empty: nothing could have made
# it TRUE. A non-corpus name is not a FALSE at all — the run aborts, pins not
# trustworthy. And a well-formed query on an argument its relation never
# carries is a vacuous green that passes forever. Article 0a closed the
# ASSERTION side only — a widening fact now aborts the run instead of loading —
# but a QUERY on an unmentioned name still answers a clean FALSE, so kind one
# is untouched. Pin a FALSE only where the KB could have said TRUE, and say in
# the comment what would flip it.

:expect-pins 45

# Hano — meant it, and that alone is not enough. These two pins belong next to
# each other: with no dimension recorded at all, severe(Hano) would be FALSE for
# want of anything to test, and the threshold would be unguarded.
? attack(Hano, Ivo).
# => TRUE
? severe(Hano).
# => FALSE

# Hano has no family entry, but family is now an inert regression input: adding
# it may not change eligibility, housing, or destination. The typed
# PlacementHome fact, not family or the legacy home fact, supports Homestay.
? family(Hano).
# => FALSE
? fit(Hano, Homestay).
# => TRUE
? dwell(Hano).
# => TRUE

# [v0.9] The where has its own atom: home confinement is a placement AT
# somewhere, recorded in the same relation as the facilities. Before the fold,
# dwell was the only trace that Hano's placement existed, and one atom carried
# "housed" and "at Homestay" at once.
? building(Homestay, Hano).
# => TRUE

# Ruk — severe, non-domestic: severity blocks eligibility, high security.
# Severity here is intent plus a second person harmed, and the second person is
# an ordinary injury entry, not a count of anything.
? attack(Ruk, Opal).
# => TRUE
? injure(Ruk, Pax).
# => TRUE
? severe(Ruk).
# => TRUE
? family(Ruk).
# => FALSE
? fit(Ruk, Homestay).
# => FALSE
? building(HighSec, Ruk).
# => TRUE

# Nando — family present, not severe, no typed placement-home option. Family
# does not route or disqualify: Homestay eligibility derives, LowSec does not,
# and no named destination is fabricated.
? family(Nando).
# => TRUE
? fit(Nando, Homestay).
# => TRUE
? severe(Nando).
# => FALSE
? building(LowSec, Nando).
# => FALSE

# Lalo — family and typed home entries present, but severe: HighSec. Severity
# comes from intent and cruelty on one act; family and home availability do not
# alter the destination.
? family(Lalo).
# => TRUE
? attack(Lalo, Mina).
# => TRUE
? cruel(Lalo, Mina).
# => TRUE
? severe(Lalo).
# => TRUE
? building(HighSec, Lalo).
# => TRUE

# Don — cruelty plus a second person harmed. Severity routes HighSec without
# any family or typed placement-home premise.
? severe(Don).
# => TRUE
? at(Don, PlacementHome).
# => FALSE
? building(HighSec, Don).
# => TRUE

# THE REPAIRED ALARM. The old rule keyed on HAVING a home and fired on Ruk and
# Lalo — both correctly placed at HighSec, flagged for the ordinary condition
# of a severe offender with a house — and on nobody misplaced. The repaired
# marker reads a placement report, the shipped record holds none, and the
# alarm is silent about everybody until the closing probes write one.
? err(Ruk, Placement).
# => FALSE
? err(Lalo, Placement).
# => FALSE
? err(Hano, Placement).
# => FALSE

# Nando has no typed placement-home fact. That absence prevents a named
# Homestay destination; only a placement report can put someone in the alarm's
# sight.
? at(Nando, PlacementHome).
# => FALSE

# THE DESTINATION-FREE CASE. Kel is non-severe and has no typed placement-home
# fact. Eligibility and housing derive, but no destination is fabricated. The
# two FALSE facility queries remain load-bearing.
? fit(Kel, Homestay).
# => TRUE
? at(Kel, PlacementHome).
# => FALSE
? building(HighSec, Kel).
# => FALSE
? building(LowSec, Kel).
# => FALSE

# ...and the fold gives them no pseudo-destination either. The state houses
# them (their dwell pins below) and the record holds no where — deliberately:
# a Homestay entry needs a home, and inventing a constant for "wherever the
# state found" would fabricate the missing destination chapter 13's sting
# turns on. If this flips TRUE, somebody wrote that constant.
? building(Homestay, Kel).
# => FALSE
? fit(Adam, Homestay).
# => TRUE

# [v0.8] "Kel and Adam are housed." Shelter now follows from being confined with
# nowhere to be confined — NOT from a placement. These two were FALSE and the
# chapter's closing section turned on their being FALSE; it now turns on their
# being TRUE, so they are the pins that say which version of the chapter is in
# print.
? dwell(Kel).
# => TRUE
? dwell(Adam).
# => TRUE

# Every confined person in the current bounded model derives shelter. These
# cast queries remain chapter-local witnesses across current routes. The
# generated placement audit owns the full subject/severity/family/home matrix,
# with family required to remain inert.
? dwell(Hano).
# => TRUE
? dwell(Ruk).
# => TRUE
? dwell(Nando).
# => TRUE
? dwell(Don).
# => TRUE

# ...and it reaches nobody who is not confined. Bela is the test: voided,
# unrecognised, and not a prisoner.
? dwell(Bela).
# => FALSE

# Family presence and absence are retained as regression facts, not placement
# premises. Nando's TRUE and Opal's FALSE cannot decide a destination.
? family(Nando).
# => TRUE
? family(Opal).
# => FALSE

# ── THE ALARM, EXERCISED. MUST BE LAST: ground facts persist ─────────────────
# One report against eligibility, one report with it — the same write, opposite
# verdicts, so the ~fit conjunct is the whole of what decides. Ruk is severe
# and ineligible; a homestay report on him is exactly the misplacement the
# marker exists to catch. Hano is eligible and already home-confined; the same
# report about him alarms nobody. Both negative-controlled against the
# pre-repair constitution, where put is not admitted vocabulary and the first
# line below aborts the run.
put(State, Ruk, Homestay).

? err(Ruk, Placement).
# => TRUE

put(State, Hano, Homestay).

? err(Hano, Placement).
# => FALSE
